<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Banten Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1527 CE – 1813 CE · capital Banten (Surosowan)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The pepper sultanate of western Java, a leading Asian port of the 17th century until the Dutch East India Company reduced it to a vassal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1527 forces from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/demak-sultanate">Demak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maulana-hasanuddin">Maulana Hasanuddin</a>, took the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom</a>. Hasanuddin became its first ruler. In 1579 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maulana-yusuf">Maulana Yusuf</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a>, ending the Sunda Kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia's</a> great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> markets, drawing Chinese, Indian, English and Danish merchants; the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet arrived in 1596, and the English opened a factory in 1602. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-ageng-tirtayasa">Sultan Ageng Tirtayasa</a>, from 1651, built canals and a navy and challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Dutch Batavia</a>. The Surosowan palace and Great Mosque date from the 16th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1682 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-ageng-tirtayasa">Ageng</a>'s son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Haji, sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> help against his father, and the Company won control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten's</a> trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels">Herman Willem Daendels</a> destroyed the palace in 1808, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a> abolished the sultanate in 1813.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banten-sultanate.jpg" alt="Banten Sultanate" width="320" height="223"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banten_Sultanate_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1527 CE – 1813 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Banten (Surosowan)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maulana Hasanuddin, Maulana Yusuf, Ageng Tirtayasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese (Banten), Sundanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Banten, Bantam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1670&amp;focus=banten-sultanate" aria-label="Banten Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7621 2403 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banten Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7941 2659L8063 2691M7941 2659L7959 2663M7941 2659L7959 2663M7941 2659L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8063 2691h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7941 2659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7941 2659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Banten. <a href="/atlas?year=1670&amp;focus=banten-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
